  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA warm front will lift north into the forecast area today,\nchasing out transient high pressure to the east. A surface low\nwill then eventually move into south central Quebec by this\nevening with a cold front extending to it's southwest. Most\nguidance suggests that we will see isolated to scattered showers\nand storms along and ahead of this frontal boundary as it\napproaches.\n\nMost of the CAMs are suggesting that storms will be ongoing\nalong the front across Vermont in the afternoon. Chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms will then be on the increase over New\nHampshire late afternoon and into the evening as this activity \napproaches. While we will have some instability to work with, \ntiming will not be diurnally optimal and storms may be decaying\nas they approach. Still, one or two storms could be on the \nstronger side with gusty winds and locally heavy downpours. The\nbest synoptic forcing for ascent will generally be across \nnorthern portions of our forecast area so northern New\nHampshire, the western Maine mountains, and the Kennebec River\nValley are most favored for the best coverage of showers and \nstorms late today/tonight.\n\nThe front should then move out fairly quickly tonight with only\nsome lingering showers over the far north through early Tuesday\nmorning.\n\nRegarding temperatures in the short term, it will be another \nvery warm day today with highs ranging from the upper 70s/lower\n80s north, to the low to mid 80s elsewhere. A few upper 80s \nwill also be possible across southeast New Hampshire and coastal\nMaine. That being said, humidity will take another step down so\nthe heat should be a bit more comfortable. Tonight's lows will\nmainly range from the mid/upper 50s north, to the low/mid 60s\nsouth.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nBroad troughing dominates the 500 mb pattern into the first \npart of the weekend with the main forecast challenge being the \ntrack of low pressure, and potentially an upper low, late in the\nweek.\n\nFlow becomes more zonal Tuesday into Wednesday as a broad \ntrough sets up over the Northeast. This will help to cool \ndewpoints, but high temperatures are still going to be able to \nclimb into the mid to upper 80s south of the mountains as 850 mb\ntemperatures remain around +15 to 16C. North of the mountains \nshould begin to see some of the cooler air advection with \ntemperatures topping out in the mid to upper 70s. These days \nshould be mostly dry as well with the exception being the \npossibility of upslope showers Tuesday as low pressure forms \nover the Gulf of St. Lawrence.\n\nPrecipitation chances than increase again Wednesday night into \nThursday as the trough axis begins to approach the region. This \nis going to be the next best chance for widespread showers and \nthunderstorms, but the finer details are uncertain at this time.\nThe Euro Ensemble suite has a tight cluster suggesting surface \nlow pressure forming over Quebec in the St. Lawrence River \nRegion, while the GFS and Canadian are more clustered over the \nGulf of St. Lawrence. The Euro also has an attendant upper low \nwhich would help to intensify things, where as the the GFS and \nCanadian currently keep an open wave. The bottom line is that \nall these solutions at least suggest showers and storms, so that\nis high confidence. The lower confidence is in coverage and \nmagnitude of the showers and storms. This will have to continue \nto be refined.\n\nThere is some uncertainty going into the first part of the \nweekend as depending on how the midweek event plays out there \ncould still be low pressure in the vicinity, but there is higher\nconfidence in general improvement as the week comes to a close.\nGlobal models are in agreement that high pressure begins to \nbuild in by Saturday and with the trough axis situated to the \neast, northwesterly flow will be bringing a cooler and drier \nairmass so the weekend could be quite a stunner with highs in \nthe 70s and low 80s under mostly clear skies.",
